# Saved data and backups

- A server's data directory is a named volume the daemon creates (`docker.NewDataVolumeName`, labelled, verified after create) and records on the row (`data_volume`); `start` creates it when the row has none or the recorded one is gone (job log line, fingerprint forgotten) and mounts it at `build.DataDir`. A restore or reset replaces it with a new name. `Delete` stops the container (`stopForData`), takes a `pre_delete` safety backup (skipped with `DeleteOptions.SkipSafetyBackup`, which the handler allows for admin sessions only), then removes container, volume and row; a failed backup removes nothing, calls `putBack` and matches `ErrSafetyBackup` (handler: 503 when it wraps `docker.ErrUnavailable`, else 409 with the job detail); a failed container removal also calls `putBack`. `RemoveVolume` refuses the current volume of any row and volumes in `pendingVolumes` (created by a running job, not yet recorded).
- Everything read from a volume is untrusted. `backups.Normalize` keeps only regular files and directories with clean relative paths (`cleanRel`: UTF-8, no controls, no `.`/`..`, bounded length and depth), each inside a kept directory, zeroes uid/gid, masks modes, drops PAX records, and has one input budget: every header (PAX/GNU records counted by `metaRecords`) counts towards `MaxEntries`, every entry's size (kept or skipped, any type) counts against `--backup-max-mb` and is refused before it is drained, and `budgetReader` bounds the raw stream to the limit plus `EntryAllowance` per entry (`errBudget` is `ErrTooLarge`; the copy is cut off, not drained), and reads only the top-level `world.snapshot` header (`ParseSnapshotHeader`, strict; "" when unknown). Skipped entries are only counted per reason (`Result.Skipped`, the `Skip*` reasons, worded by `DescribeSkipped` for the job log); their names never leave the package (the game chose them, they can hold environment values); `Result.SkippedCount` is stored as `omitted_entries`. `Rewrite` re-checks a stored archive entry by entry against the recorded counts before `docker cp` in, with uid/gid 65532.
- Archives: `backups.Store` writes `.tmp-*`, syncs, renames to `<32 hex>.tar.gz`, and only then the caller inserts the row; rows are deleted before files; `Sweep` (startup, before jobs) removes temp files and archives without a row. `Acquire` marks a backup read by a running restore; `Delete` refuses it (`ErrInUse` → 409). Free space keeps `staticfiles.DefaultReserveBytes` (approximate: checked every `checkEvery`, not coordinated between backups). `OpenFile` opens without following links (Lstat regular file, `O_NOFOLLOW` on unix, `os.SameFile` with the opened file); `OpenVerified` verifies size and SHA-256 on that open file and rewinds it: restore copies from it, download serves the `OpenFile` result.
- Helpers: every volume read or write goes through `createHelper` (verified build image, `docker.HelperSpec`; a backup uses `helperImage` over `dataSource`: the recorded container's build, then the assigned build), never started, removed in a deferred `removeHelper` (also on failure and cancellation). The launch id is recorded (`volume_helpers`, synced) before the create and the container id after; A create that fails without a validation error goes to `abandonHelper`, which keeps the record when nothing is found; `CleanupHelpers` (startup and every schedule tick) removes only recorded helpers not in `activeHelpers` and forgets a record without a container id whose helper is not found only after `helperForgetAfter`; `knownHelpers` keeps them out of foreign-container logging. Never act on a helper found by labels alone.
- `stopForData` counts a container as running before the stop only when the server's desired state is `running` and the container is running or restarting; when the stop fails or is cancelled it lets the stop finish (a second stop under its own deadline) before `putBack`. `Delete` honours cancellation up to the container removal, then carries on without it and records the server as stopped. Docker's output about a failed copy is withheld from logs (it can name files of the game): pass the `*docker.CmdError` itself to `failDocker`, never an error that wraps it.
- Jobs: `Backup` (`server.backup`), `Restore` (`server.restore`, admin, restricted) and `ResetData` (`server.data_reset`, admin, restricted) run under the server subject. Provenance (`dataSource`): build, commit and engine version from the recorded container's labels, verified against the build row and image, else the assigned build. A live backup calls `gameadmin.Client.Snapshot` under one `SnapshotDeadline` (409 waits `SnapshotRetry`, re-inspects the recorded container under the same deadline and uses the port of that inspection; 503 fails "try again shortly"; 501 or no answer copies as is; other failures fail). Job failure causes are sentinels only: file system errors go through `failFS` to the daemon log. Restore: `checkRestore` in the handler (`CheckRestore`) and again in the job (keeping the verified open archive); `stopForData` (notes whether the recorded container ran, stops it without changing desired state, calls `putBack` when the stop itself fails or is cancelled); `safetyBackup` (`pre_restore`/`pre_reset`/`pre_delete`: skipped only without volume or entries; omitted entries store the backup when it has a file and then fail with `ErrSafetyBackup`); fresh volume plus helper; `copyIn` refuses (`errImageData`) when the helper's fresh volume already holds image content (Docker copy-up; `volume-nocopy` cannot be used because `docker cp` never applies a tar's `.` entry to the mount root, see `TestIntegrationRestoreVolumeRoot`); a cancellation check; then one `SwitchServerData` write; then `afterSwitch` (bookkeeping without cancellation, old container and volume removal, start unless a forced mismatch or a cancellation, never ending as cancelled). Every exit before the switch (or, on delete, before the container is removed) leaves the old volume recorded and calls `putBack`: when the recorded container ran before the stop and does not run now, `docker start` of that recorded id (`StartServer`), then `waitRecordedReady` with the recorded admin token; it never creates or removes a container or reads the assigned build, runs under a non-cancelled context bounded by `StartTimeout` plus `restartAllowance`, and reports only to the job log. `putback_test.go` injects every exit.
- Schema guard: `schemaMismatch(data, build)` is true only when both are known and differ. `launch` (behind `start`) checks it right after reading the build and the volume state, before `removePending` and any other container operation. `launch` sets `*replaced` right before `removeRecorded`; `Recover` stops the recorded container only when it was set, so every earlier failure leaves it as it was. `ready` records the game's fingerprint on the row (`SetServerDataFingerprint`, only for the volume it started with) and on the build only through `recordBuildFingerprint` (`FillBuildFingerprint`; a differing report is a job log warning). Builds read the committed fingerprint at build time (see the builds rule).
- Schedule: `ScheduleBackups` (daemon loop every `ScheduleInterval`) starts `scheduledBackup` jobs for running servers whose newest backup is older than the interval, skipping servers whose last scheduled backup failed within `ScheduleRetryDelay` (`servers.backup_failed_at`, survives restarts); the job body re-checks `stillDue` after it holds the job slot (also `backup_failed_at`, which a failing job may have written after the tick read the row; an error of the re-check other than cancellation records a failure time); retention deletes only `scheduled` backups beyond `keep`.
- Handlers decode and check (note, `backup_id`, server exists, no active job) and map errors in `serversError` (`ErrBackupNotFound` 404, `ErrBackupInUse`/`ErrBackupDamaged`/`ErrNoBuild`/`ErrVolumeCurrent` 409 conflict, `ErrSafetyBackup` on delete 503 with `docker.ErrUnavailable`, else 409 with the job detail, `*SchemaMismatchError` 409 `schema_mismatch`). Downloads use `http.ServeContent` with an attachment name built from `[a-z0-9-]` only.
- Verify with `go test ./internal/backups/... ./internal/servers/... ./internal/store/... ./internal/httpapi/... ./internal/docker/... ./internal/gameadmin/...`; with Docker `GOLEM_DEPLOY_DOCKER_TESTS=1 go test -p 1 ./internal/docker/... ./internal/httpapi/...` (`TestIntegrationDataVolumes`, `TestIntegrationRestoreVolumeRoot`, `TestIntegrationBackupsAPI` with the fake game's snapshot endpoint).
